hi there Steve Kaufman haven't done a  video in a while i've i've had that  family visiting my son and his family  from the UK my brother from toronto  kicked out of my office so i'm doing it  from a different location and I thought  I would do a video here because I had a  question from I think he calls himself  the seductive arts one of my viewers who  said you know how do you deal with the  problem of learning some of the basic  vocabulary because I think he's learning  Italian and French and he listens to a  lot of news and so he has a lot of the  vocabulary that relates to news and  politics and doesn't know the name of  everyday items in those languages and  what should I do and and this is a  subject that comes up all the time  someone mentioned before that they had  been studying through a lot of listening  and reading and then they went somewhere  and it took a course and the teacher  said you know your case is quite typical  you don't know the names of the most  common things in the language and the  other day I was talking to my check  tutor Andre and he started talking about  food and said now we're going to talk  about the names of the different food  stuffs are you know items on a menu or  food products in check and I said stop  you know I won't be able to learn them  because I don't have a daily need for  those words and so on the subject of how  do we learn the basic vocabulary I  believe that we learn the vocabulary  that's important to us and it's not  necessarily the names of different food  items that you're going to buy in a  store or in a restaurant it's not  necessarily the name for comb or  refrigerator or even basic things in  daily life if you're not at in fact  living in the language you don't need  those basic words if i lived in the  czech republic and if i was eating every  day and going to the store every day  then i would quickly learn those words  but it's pointless for me I believed to  have a lesson on the  a different you know names of food  dishes in check if I won't encounter  those words again for you know months so  to me because I'm interested in  following the new following the news  then economics terms political terms a  lot of terms about you know in that  regard or from that point of view or in  my view and these kinds of words that  come up in a discussion are easier for  me to learn because they come up more  often and I don't think there's any  particular benefit in trying to teach  the so-called basic words the most  common words will come up in any case no  matter what we're using as learning  content and it's also true that in the  beginning we tend to learn terms like  hello how are you my name is when why  we're who and these will come up anyway  and it is helpful in the beginning to  use simpler shorter dialogues but I  personally find that I find move along  quickly to more demanding content it's  still going to be seventy eighty percent  very common vocabulary so I'm continuing  to reinforce my grasp of a lot of a  common vocabulary and I'm developing  specialized vocabulary in areas of  interest and as that area of interest  changes or as my needs change then i'll  have an opportunity to learn other  vocabulary and it has never bothered me  that in a language where i might be able  to discuss economics i might be quite  clueless in everyday things when i lived  in japan I could do business in Japanese  my wife who certainly couldn't do  business in Japanese was much better at  the store buying things you know  vegetables and fruit than she knew the  names of all these things and  communicated very comfortably with a  grocer so you learn the language that  you need for your particular situation  so I'm not a big believer in this idea  of the basic vocabulary it'll come  anyway and north any more than I'm a  believer in  learning the basic grammar first I mean  as I've said before you can have a sense  of what the what some of the grammatical  issues are but the idea that you could  nail down tenses or nailed down cases  early on and your language learning is  is not certainly not the way I learned  so I just thought I'd do that because I  had a request from the seductive arts or  whatever his name was on the subject of  what to do when you seem to have these  gaps in your vocabulary which often are  some very basic words that's perfectly  normal in my opinion and if you you know  if you have a method of learning which  which basically enables you to learn  from things of interest to you or you're  always doing meaningful things in the  language you don't have an artificial  dialogue about someone's refrigerator  you are basically doing things that  interest you or you're engaged in  discussions around things that are  meaningful to you you will learn the  vocabulary that is useful for that  context and as these contexts change and  as other words come up more often in  your reading listening speaking then  you'll learn those words if you have a  solid base in the language that's never  too late and it won't be difficult to  learn the names for pen pencil  refrigerator exercise book or whatever  other so-called basic words you may need  so there you have it thank you for  listening
 

